#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Injects the development-flow map at session start: which entry-point skill
# governs which moment. It is a static reference table, so it lives here rather
# than in CLAUDE.md, which carries standing preferences.
set -euo pipefail

cat >/dev/null # payload unused (the map is static); drain stdin anyway

jq_bin=$(command -v jq || true)
[ -n "$jq_bin" ] || exit 0

flow_map=$(cat <<'EOF'
Development flow — the entry-point skill for each moment:
- Creative work ahead (a feature, design, or architecture choice): settle the
  design interactively via [[skill:brainstorming]]; capture it in a [[skill:specs]] file.
- Building non-trivial code from that design: [[skill:code-writing-flow]] — you
  drive the stages in order (plan, your call on it, pseudocode, explain it to me,
  verify, code, review, docs, doc review, comments, comment style).
- Implementing directly, or inside the code stage: [[skill:test-driven-development]]
  (RED-GREEN-REFACTOR).
- A bug or unexpected failure: [[skill:systematic-debugging]] — find the root
  cause before any fix, never patch blind.
- Code-review feedback arrives: [[skill:receiving-code-review]] — verify before
  implementing, no performative agreement.
- About to claim something is done, fixed, or passing:
  [[skill:verification-before-completion]] — evidence, never "should".
- A development branch is complete: [[skill:finishing-a-development-branch]] —
  tests first, then merge locally / keep / discard; never push or open a PR
  unprompted.
See [[skill:using-skills]] for how skills are discovered and linked.
EOF
)

"$jq_bin" -n --arg content "$flow_map" \
  '{hookSpecificOutput: {hookEventName: "SessionStart", additionalContext: $content}}'
